  "worked_solution": "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that if there are $x$ *vacant* rooms, the hotel has $(71-x)$ occupied rooms, and the rate per room is $(50+2x)$ dollars. So, the total revenue (in dollars) is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={{(71-x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{occupied rooms}}\\times \\,\\,\\,{{(50+2x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{rate per room}} \\\\  & =3\\,550+142x-50x-2{x}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\,550+92x-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, for having $(71-x)$ rooms rented out, the hotel has a cost (in dollars) of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(x) & =4(71-x) \\\\  & =284-4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\ P(x) & =3\\,550+92x-2{x}^{2}-(284-4x) \\\\  & =3\\,266+96x-2{x}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 71.$ \n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<71$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=96-4x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 96-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x & =96 \\\\ x & =24\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=24$ lies in the interval $0<x<71.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=24$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-4<0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=24$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=24$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that the hotel will have a maximum profit level when there are $x=24$ vacant rooms. \n\nWe know that the rate per room is $(50+2x)$ dollars. If there are $24$ vacant rooms, then the rate per room is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}50+2(24) & =$98.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the manager should charge a room rate of $$98$ per night.",
